def levenshtein_within(a: str, b: str, k: int) -> bool:
    if abs(len(a) - len(b)) > k:
        return False
    if len(a) < len(b):
        a, b = b, a
    prev = list(range(len(b) + 1))
    for i, ca in enumerate(a, 1):
        curr = [i] + [0] * len(b)
        min_in_row = curr[0]
        for j, cb in enumerate(b, 1):
            cost = 0 if ca == cb else 1
            curr[j] = min(curr[j-1] + 1, prev[j] + 1, prev[j-1] + cost)
            if curr[j] < min_in_row:
                min_in_row = curr[j]
        if min_in_row > k:
            return False
        prev = curr
    return prev[-1] <= k
```
If every cell in the current row exceeds k, no future row can drop back below k. Early-exit can save large amounts of work when k is small.

